<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baalbek</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Heliopolis (Syria)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baalbek is a town in the Beqaa valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, known in antiquity as Heliopolis. Its Roman temple complex, with the temples of Jupiter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a> and Venus built between the 1st and 3rd centuries, is one of the largest in the Roman world. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the sanctuary was fortified as a citadel, and the town was held by Ayyubid princes and later by the Harfush <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baalbek.jpg" alt="Baalbek" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lodo from Moscow, Russia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Bacchus,_Baalbek,_Lebanon_(49890013476).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
